# Frontend Performance (CSS & Animations)

Rules to keep the UI responsive and avoid sluggishness or low frames per second. Follow these when adding or changing styles, especially in grids, cards, modals, and scrollable sections.

## Avoid Expensive Effects

### Backdrop blur

- **Do not** use `backdrop-blur` (or `backdrop-blur-sm`, `backdrop-blur-md`, etc.) on multiple elements in the same view (e.g. every card in a grid). Blur is composited and repainted often; it is a major cause of low FPS.
- **Prefer** solid or semi-transparent backgrounds (e.g. `bg-purple-500/80`, `bg-black/40`) for the same visual weight without the cost.
- **If** you need a single “glass” accent (e.g. one hero badge or one modal overlay), one `backdrop-blur` is acceptable; avoid it on repeated list/grid items.

### Scroll-smooth on content sections

- **Do not** add `scroll-smooth` to content sections or cards. It can make scrolling feel heavy and hurt FPS when that section is in view.
- **Prefer** smooth scrolling only where the user explicitly triggers it (e.g. “Scroll to section” button) via `element.scrollTo({ behavior: 'smooth' })` in JavaScript. Do not put `scroll-smooth` on the section element itself.

## Prefer Explicit Transitions

### Avoid transition-all

- **Do not** use `transition-all` for hover or state changes. It animates every animatable property and can trigger extra layout and paint.
- **Prefer** scoped transition utilities, e.g.:
    - `transition-opacity duration-300` when only opacity changes
    - `transition-colors duration-200` when only colors change
    - `transition-[transform,opacity]` when you need both and nothing else

```html
<!-- ❌ Avoid: animates everything -->
<button class="transition-all duration-300 ... ...">
    <!-- ✅ Prefer: only what changes -->
    <div class="transition-opacity duration-300 ... ..."></div>
</button>
```

## Paint containment

- For **repeated blocks** that form a grid or list (e.g. project cards, news cards), consider adding paint containment so the browser can limit repaints to that block.
- In Tailwind you can use an arbitrary value, e.g. `[contain:paint]`, on the repeated element’s root (e.g. the card wrapper). Use only where it doesn’t break layout (e.g. avoid on elements that need to overflow or affect layout outside the box).

## Summary

| Avoid                            | Prefer                                                                    |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `backdrop-blur` on many elements | Solid or semi-transparent backgrounds; at most one blur per view          |
| `scroll-smooth` on sections      | JS `scrollTo({ behavior: 'smooth' })` when user triggers scroll           |
| `transition-all`                 | `transition-opacity`, `transition-colors`, or explicit `transition-[...]` |
| (optional) none                  | `[contain:paint]` on repeated grid/list item roots                        |

## Global CSS

- **Check `resources/css/app.css`** when the whole page or a section feels heavy: global class names (e.g. `.project-card`, `.modal-overlay`) may add `transition: all` or `backdrop-filter` even if the component no longer uses them. Prefer **transform-only** or **property-specific** transitions and avoid **backdrop-filter** in global overlay/card styles.
- Prefer defining transitions in the component (Tailwind) so they stay explicit and easy to audit; avoid duplicating or overriding with broad transitions in app.css.

Apply these when writing or reviewing Vue templates, Tailwind, and app.css so the projects section and similar areas stay smooth and high FPS.
